## Derivation of the Forward Equation with Mortality

### Setup

Let $X(s)$ be a one-dimensional diffusion process governed by the SDE

$$dX(s) = b(X(s),s)\,ds + \sigma(X(s),s)\,dW(s), \qquad a(y,s)=\sigma^2(y,s),$$

starting from $X(t)=x$. The process is additionally subject to a **mortality (killing) mechanism** at rate $\psi$. Define the **survival-weighted transition density**

$$f(y,s,t)\,dy \;=\; \mathbb{E}\!\left[\exp\!\Bigl(-\int_t^s \psi\,du\Bigr)\,\mathbf{1}_{\{X(s)\in[y,y+dy]\}}\;\Big|\;X(t)=x\right],$$

i.e. $f(y,s,t)$ is the sub-probability density of being at $y$ at time $s$ **and** having survived (not been killed) from $t$ to $s$.

---

### Step 1 — Itô / Feynman–Kac identity for a test function

Let $\varphi(y)$ be any smooth, compactly supported test function. By the definition of $f$,

$$\int_{\mathbb{R}} \varphi(y)\,f(y,s,t)\,dy \;=\; \mathbb{E}_{x,t}\!\left[\exp\!\Bigl(-\int_t^s \psi\,du\Bigr)\,\varphi\bigl(X(s)\bigr)\right]. \tag{1}$$

Apply Itô's formula to $\varphi(X(s))$ and the product rule to the exponential factor. Setting $M(u)=\exp\!\bigl(-\int_t^u \psi\,dv\bigr)$, we get

$$d\bigl[M(u)\,\varphi(X(u))\bigr] = M(u)\Bigl[\underbrace{b(y,u)\,\varphi'(y)+\tfrac12 a(y,u)\,\varphi''(y)}_{=:\,L_u\varphi(y)}-\psi\,\varphi(y)\Bigr]_{y=X(u)}du + d(\text{martingale}).$$

Integrating from $t$ to $s$, taking expectations (the martingale vanishes), and using $M(t)=1$, $\varphi(X(t))=\varphi(x)$:

$$\int \varphi(y)\,f(y,s,t)\,dy \;=\; \varphi(x) + \int_t^s\!\int \varphi(y)\Bigl[L_u\varphi(y)-\psi\,\varphi(y)\Bigr]\,f(y,u,t)\,dy\,du. \tag{2}$$

---

### Step 2 — Integration by parts (transfer derivatives from $\varphi$ to $f$)

The operator inside (2) is

$$L_u\varphi(y)-\psi\,\varphi(y)=b(y,u)\,\varphi'(y)+\tfrac12 a(y,u)\,\varphi''(y)-\psi\,\varphi(y).$$

Since $\varphi$ has compact support, boundary terms vanish and integration by parts gives:

$$\int b(y,u)\,\varphi'(y)\,f\,dy = -\int \varphi(y)\,\frac{\partial}{\partial y}\bigl[b(y,u)\,f\bigr]\,dy,$$

$$\int \tfrac12 a(y,u)\,\varphi''(y)\,f\,dy = \int \varphi(y)\,\tfrac12\frac{\partial^2}{\partial y^2}\bigl[a(y,u)\,f\bigr]\,dy,$$

$$\int \psi\,\varphi(y)\,f\,dy = \int \varphi(y)\,\psi\,f\,dy \qquad (\text{multiplication, self-adjoint}).$$

Substituting into (2):

$$\int \varphi(y)\,f(y,s,t)\,dy = \varphi(x)+\int_t^s\!\int \varphi(y)\underbrace{\left[\tfrac12\bigl(a(y,u)f\bigr)_{yy}-\bigl(b(y,u)f\bigr)_y-\psi\,f\right]}_{=:\,(\mathcal{L}_u^* f)(y,u,t)}dy\,du. \tag{3}$$

---

### Step 3 — Differentiate in $s$ and conclude

Differentiating (3) with respect to the forward time $s$:

$$\frac{\partial}{\partial s}\int \varphi(y)\,f(y,s,t)\,dy = \int \varphi(y)\Bigl[\tfrac12\bigl(a(y,s)f\bigr)_{yy}-\bigl(b(y,s)f\bigr)_y-\psi\,f\Bigr]dy.$$

Since $\varphi$ is an **arbitrary** test function, the integrands must agree pointwise:

$$\boxed{\;f_s = \tfrac12\bigl(a(y,s)\,f\bigr)_{yy} - \bigl(b(y,s)\,f\bigr)_y - \psi\,f.\;}$$

When the mortality rate is evaluated at the **initial** position, $\psi=\psi(x)$ (a constant with respect to $y$ and $s$), this becomes exactly

$$f_s = \tfrac12\bigl(a(y,s)\,f\bigr)_{yy} - \bigl(b(y,s)\,f\bigr)_y - \psi(x)\,f.$$

---

### Step 4 — Initial condition

At $s=t$ the process is concentrated at its starting point $X(t)=x$, and the survival weight is $M(t)=1$, so

$$f(y,t,t)=\delta(y-x).$$

One may verify consistency: $\int f(y,t,t)\,dy=1$ (certain survival at the initial instant), and $\int f(y,s,t)\,dy = e^{-\psi(x)(s-t)}\le 1$ for $s>t$, reflecting the probability of survival up to time $s$.

---

### Summary

The forward equation is the **adjoint (Fokker–Planck) form** of the extended generator $L_u\varphi - \psi\,\varphi$, obtained by:

1. Writing the Feynman–Kac / Itô identity (2) for an arbitrary test function $\varphi$;
2. Transferring all $y$-derivatives from $\varphi$ onto $f$ via integration by parts (the adjoint operation);
3. Using the arbitrariness of $\varphi$ to equate integrands.

The diffusion contributes the second-order term $\tfrac12(a f)_{yy}$, the drift contributes the first-order term $-(b f)_y$, and the mortality contributes the zeroth-order (absorption) term $-\psi f$. The delta initial condition encodes the deterministic start $X(t)=x$.

